WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.120
- I've been very blessed to have had

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:05.160
some absolutely amazing
friendships in my life.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.630
And while many of them have come and gone,

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:10.090
some of them got married,
some of them moved town,

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.081
one of them became a priest, actually.

00:00:12.081 --> 00:00:13.648
(crowd laughing)

00:00:13.648 --> 00:00:14.940
But anyways.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.720
All the amazing friends in my
life have taught me something

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.920
about what it means to be me.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.830
Because I think you view
yourself through the eyes

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:25.490
of other people, especially
people you're close with.

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:27.980
So I think it's really
important to surround yourself

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.840
with the right people, to form
your tribe with intention.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.480
You wanna surround yourself with people

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.070
who make you a better person.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.440
And for the most part those
types of relationships

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:38.800
are gonna be friendships,

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.830
but you don't get quality
friendships by following

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:44.640
a step-by-step action plan
or something like that.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.580
Some of the best
relationships I've ever had

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.490
in my life seem to have
just randomly happened

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:51.810
when I wasn't really
expecting it whatsoever.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.580
So needless to say with this video

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.670
I'm not interested in giving
you a step-by-step guide

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.310
as to how to make everyone
want to be your friend,

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:00.260
or anything like that,

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:03.120
because I think that's
kind of futile and dumb.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.720
But I do wanna talk about
some really useful things

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.370
that I've learned throughout my life,

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:09.580
that have really helped
me make friends easier

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:11.470
and make higher quality friends.

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:14.981
And overall just connect with
people a lot more genuinely.

00:01:14.981 --> 00:01:17.455
♪ Somebody sexy, tell 'em hey ♪

00:01:17.455 --> 00:01:18.400
♪ Give me ev-- ♪

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.160
- The number one tip that I have is to

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.170
always assume initiative.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.920
So back when I was like 15 or 16 years old

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.590
I was browsing Reddit
self-improvement forums

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:28.590
and stuff like that.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:32.460
I was on like r/socialskills
and I came across this post

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:34.820
that pretty much changed my entire life.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:36.750
I've been looking everywhere
for this one post,

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:37.880
but I can't find it anywhere.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.060
So if someone can help me out
with this, that'd be great.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:42.680
But in the post, there was
this guy who talked about

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.927
how he didn't have very many friends,

00:01:44.927 --> 00:01:47.990
and the friends that he did
have were starting to fade away

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:49.550
and he didn't know why.

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:51.580
He was kind of a loner,
kind of an introvert,

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:54.410
and all these friends started
to just drift away from him,

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:55.750
and he was getting really upset.

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:58.430
Until the very last friend that he had

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.200
finally stopped calling him.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.030
Angry, confused, and saddened by this,

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:05.220
this Reddit poster runs up to his friend

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:06.987
and says, "Dude, where'd you go?

00:02:06.987 --> 00:02:08.277
"Why'd you stop calling me?

00:02:08.277 --> 00:02:10.550
"Why don't you invite
me to stuff anymore?"

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.407
And his friend turned
to him and simply said,

00:02:13.407 --> 00:02:15.027
"It's 'cause you don't invite me anywhere.

00:02:15.027 --> 00:02:17.380
"I figured you didn't
like hanging out with me."

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:20.530
And this really kind of
changed my perspective

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:24.250
because I just never
invited people anywhere.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:26.610
I always waited for the invitation.

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:28.570
I was always sitting there on my butt,

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:30.720
wondering why nobody was inviting me

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.200
to cool parties and stuff.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:33.260
And I eventually realized

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.760
that I wasn't making any effort whatsoever

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.650
to make plans and call people
up, invite them to stuff.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:41.220
If you don't have very many friends

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:44.210
and you tend to not do things very often

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:46.690
and you wanna be someone
who does things with people,

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:47.900
then take control.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:49.850
Just like any other aspect of your life,

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:51.670
if you wanna change
something it's up to you

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:52.760
because it's your life.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:54.850
So take control of your
social life as well.

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:56.660
Always assume initiative.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:58.290
If you wanna do something fun,

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:01.660
come up with an idea, invite
the person to that idea.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:04.700
If they don't wanna come
to that idea, leave 'em be.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:05.890
Is that recording?

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:07.540
- [Man] I recorded you peeing.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:08.373
- Did you?

00:03:08.373 --> 00:03:09.206
- [Man] Yeah.

00:03:09.206 --> 00:03:10.480
- Okay, so the next big
thing that I've learned

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.940
is that honesty is the antidote.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:16.390
One of the biggest pitfalls
in any social interaction

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:20.950
is the fear of voicing your
opinion, or being controversial,

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:23.410
or politically incorrect, or offensive.

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:25.450
But this fear really has to go

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:28.500
if anybody is to know who
you are in this world.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.760
Because so many people are so agreeable,

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:33.960
they're extremely careful about
every little thing they say,

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.350
they're making sure they're
not hurting anyone's feelings.

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:37.810
They're making sure that they're laughing

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:38.810
at everyone's jokes.

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:39.970
They're being very pleasant.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:42.720
And what this eventually does
is that you tend to be seen

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:47.340
by a large amount of people
as, oh yeah, he's nice.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:49.590
People are kinda, they
don't really know you

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.300
but you're unoffensive to them

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:52.860
so you're okay to have around.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:56.580
You'll be liked by everyone,
but loved by no one.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:57.830
So if you're the kind of person

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.520
who doesn't ever want to
make any sort of waves,

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:02.530
or make anyone feel uncomfortable,

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:05.230
and you find it really
hard to make it past

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:07.520
kind of a surface level acquaintanceship

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:10.170
with most people, then
that's probably the reason.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:12.540
By not voicing your true opinions,

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:14.760
by not speaking honestly about things

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:17.810
that you're passionate about,
or things that you've learned,

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:19.470
or standing up for what's right,

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:22.750
you're basically concealing
your identity from people.

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:25.030
And people don't tend to bond with people

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:26.940
that they don't know at all.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:28.780
Especially people who they don't trust.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:31.170
I feel like everybody has
a few people in their life

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.520
that when they come across
them in a grocery store,

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:35.353
or something like that,

00:04:35.353 --> 00:04:37.897
you have the exact same
conversation every single time.

00:04:37.897 --> 00:04:39.527
"Hey man, how's it goin'?

00:04:39.527 --> 00:04:43.447
"Oh yeah that's cool, how's
the hockey team goin'?

00:04:43.447 --> 00:04:44.337
"Oh yeah, sweet yeah.

00:04:44.337 --> 00:04:46.817
"So you gettin' on the ice a lot?

00:04:46.817 --> 00:04:48.807
"Oh, that's good man, that's good man.

00:04:48.807 --> 00:04:51.357
"Yeah, your wife's family's all good?

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:53.077
"Oh, that's good, that's good.

00:04:53.077 --> 00:04:55.157
"Well, I'm gonna let you
go I gotta get running,

00:04:55.157 --> 00:04:56.477
"but great running into you,

00:04:56.477 --> 00:04:57.850
"I'll see you some other time."

00:04:57.850 --> 00:04:59.460
I feel like I have that conversation

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:02.110
with like eight to 10 people in my life.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:04.020
Usually just people I play hockey with

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:04.853
or something like that.

00:05:04.853 --> 00:05:07.110
So I'm not saying you need
to get past that stage

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:09.360
with every single person
that you meet in this world,

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.540
and form deep friendships with
everybody you lock eyes with.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:15.350
But what I am saying is
that if you're interested

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:18.760
in forming deeper
relationships with people

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:20.560
then you have to be willing to kinda

00:05:23.573 --> 00:05:25.300
break out of that small talk,

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.570
ask questions you're
actually curious about,

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:30.480
and that you might think is
like a weird thing to ask,

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:33.580
but if you wanna ask it, just
get good at just asking it.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:36.250
When you say something
that's controversial

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:39.480
and polarizing, people
can do either two things.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.850
They can either really bond
with what you're saying

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:43.577
and be like, "Yeah, man, that's awesome.

00:05:43.577 --> 00:05:45.060
"I totally agree."

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:47.687
Or they can be like, "I'm outta here.

00:05:47.687 --> 00:05:48.880
"This guy's cracked."

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:51.570
And both of those things
are a really good thing

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:53.150
because of my next point,

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:56.570
which is rejection is a very useful tool.

00:05:56.570 --> 00:05:58.420
When you communicate honestly,

00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:00.600
when you wear your
opinions on your sleeve,

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:02.820
when you're not afraid to be controversial

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:05.120
and be who you are in
front of other people,

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:07.300
then you're inevitably
gonna make a lot of people

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:10.570
run the other way because they
completely disagree with you.

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.370
And that's a very good thing

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:14.710
because you can find out very quickly

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:17.327
who the people who are
actually staying are.

00:06:17.327 --> 00:06:19.590
And the people who are
staying are probably people

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:21.240
who you can actually bond with.

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:22.750
And I was thinking about
this the other day,

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:26.340
because I think rejection
is like an inevitable thing

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:29.120
that always happens if
people are incompatible.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.730
And it can either happen right away,

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:33.430
or it happens very
gradually over the course

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:35.980
of a very painful and toxic relationship.

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:39.200
A quick rejection is one where
one person starts talking

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:40.910
and the other person starts talking,

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:43.220
you don't vibe with that person at all

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:44.400
so you kinda go the other way

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.260
and you never talk with that person again.

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.000
That's an acute rejection.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.040
From a dating perspective,

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:52.100
you walk up to a girl, or a guy,

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:54.457
or whoever you're approaching
and you're all like,

00:06:54.457 --> 00:06:55.510
"Hey baby."

00:06:55.510 --> 00:06:57.477
And then they're like,
"Nah, get out of here.

00:06:57.477 --> 00:07:01.610
"You suck, you smell, your
breath is bad, I don't like you."

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:02.950
And then that's it.

00:07:02.950 --> 00:07:04.420
You're like, ah okay, that kinda sucks.

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:06.740
But fair enough, I should
probably brush my teeth.

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:08.350
That's an acute rejection,

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:10.300
and you never have to talk
with that person again.

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:12.980
But that's what happens when
people are being honest.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:14.270
They're saying what they mean.

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:15.127
One person's like,

00:07:15.127 --> 00:07:17.090
"Hey, I'm interested in bonding with you."

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:19.250
The other person's like, "No, go away."

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:20.690
And then they split up.

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:22.500
I think that's absolutely perfect.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:26.060
It's honest, communication,
clear cut results.

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:28.130
But a gradual rejection is something

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:30.390
that people don't really
think about a lot.

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:33.100
And I think it's when two
people are incompatible

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:36.620
but neither of them are very
good at communicating honestly.

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:38.780
So these two types of
people might actually

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:41.640
get into a friendship or a relationship,

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:43.790
and then find out later down the road

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:46.470
that they actually don't
really like each other at all,

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:48.710
and they resent tons of things about them.

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:50.730
So they start to become
bitter at each other,

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:53.700
and start to get mad at their
fundamental differences.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:56.470
Start to get petty about
little things like,

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:58.180
if they leave a banana on the table

00:07:58.180 --> 00:07:59.620
and you lash out at them.

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:01.150
But you're not really
lashing out of the banana,

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:03.620
you're lashing out of the
fact that you disagree

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:05.520
about religious and political things.

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:07.000
But you never sorted it out to begin with

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:09.480
and now you have to deal with
all this like drama later.

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.000
It's just an absolute mess.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:12.500
But usually that only happens

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:15.020
because two parties aren't
really communicating

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:16.980
very honestly at the beginning.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:19.700
They can't see the
differences right off the bat,

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:21.560
evaluate them, and then realize,

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:23.750
you know what, this person's
so much different than me,

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:25.990
This is probably gonna cause
a lot of pain and turmoil

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:27.880
down the road so I'm just gonna split

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:29.090
before it gets too deep.

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:30.840
So if someone's incompatible with you,

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.490
rejection and no matter what.

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:35.670
It can either happen right
away, or way down the road.

00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:39.193
So opt for honest communication
and quick rejection.

00:08:39.193 --> 00:08:40.038
(chuckling)

00:08:40.038 --> 00:08:42.280
This is probably the
most un-fun, unflattering

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:44.290
social interaction advice of all time.

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:45.570
Hey, it's Better Ideas here,

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:47.960
here's how to get rejected
nine times outta 10.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:51.360
So this last bit of advice
is all about demographics.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:53.537
And I picked up this
bit of information from

00:08:53.537 --> 00:08:55.480
"Models" by Mark Manson,

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:57.960
which is actually a book about dating,

00:08:57.960 --> 00:08:59.820
and relationships, and stuff like that.

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:02.160
But this honestly applies to friendships

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:06.510
or any kind of relationship
that you want to enter into.

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:07.740
So a lot of people complain

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:09.683
that they can't meet anyone like them.

00:09:09.683 --> 00:09:11.710
They're a special little snowflake,

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:13.990
they have their own little
interests and hobbies.

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:15.710
Maybe they have some weird hobbies,

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:18.090
and interests, and
pretty strange opinions.

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:20.090
And they lament because
they can never find

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:22.040
their tribe, their people.

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:24.600
People who think exactly like them,

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:27.110
people they can just hit it
off with and have a great time.

00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:30.030
They can spew all their bizarre opinions

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:32.310
about whatever, together.

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:33.520
But people get sad because

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:35.430
they can't find people who get them.

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:39.290
Well, a lot of people look
in the most general places,

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:42.370
the most general public venues of all time

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:44.980
for their soulmate, their best friend.

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:48.350
They're not actually evaluating
what interests they have,

00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:49.940
what values they hold,

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:52.530
and going to the places
where those people exist.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:54.620
You want to increase your chances

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:56.830
of finding people that you connect with.

00:09:56.830 --> 00:09:59.410
That's just basic
demographics, it's statistics.

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:00.243
Come on.

00:10:00.243 --> 00:10:03.600
If you're religious start
actually going to that church

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:06.320
young adults group that
you've been avoiding.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:09.380
There are a lot of people who
think like you in that group.

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:11.520
If you're into sports,
you're really athletic,

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:14.690
then join an intramural
or communal sports team.

00:10:14.690 --> 00:10:17.670
Start to get involved with
the extracurricular events

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:21.130
and the little optional group
meetups and stuff like that.

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:22.490
Go where your people are.

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:24.160
There are people who share your hobbies

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:26.000
and your passions, go there.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:29.220
But sometimes you might not
have a crazy proficiency

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:31.290
in every single one of your hobbies.

